      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The site was once a plantation owned by the Eskridge family, where Mary Ball Washington grew up under Colonel George Eskridge’s guardianship.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sandy_point_westmoreland_county_virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1962 a Virginia historical marker titled “Sandy Point” was erected along County Route 614 to commemorate its colonial ties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sandy_point_westmoreland_county_virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Sandy Point’s low‑lying sandy terrain sits at elevations near sea level, making it part of the Northern Neck’s tidal marsh ecosystem.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sandy_point_westmoreland_county_virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community lies about 5.3 miles east of Cople Highway, accessible mainly by the paved Sandy Point Road (CR 614).",
